//? Title: The Berlekamp–Massey Algorithm
//? Subtitle: Shortest linear feedback shift registers and linear complexity, used to break an LFSR stream cipher that encrypts the Old Faithful data and to measure how "linear" a real binary sequence is, using datamunge's algorithms::berlekamp_massey
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The Berlekamp–Massey algorithm finds the shortest linear feedback shift register (LFSR) that
generates a sequence. Its length is the sequence's *linear complexity*. We derive the algorithm
from Massey's lemma, derive the jump distribution \(2^{-k}\) behind the \(n/2\) profile of random
bits, and put it to work on `dm::datasets::faithful()`. Written as CSV and encrypted with a 31-bit
LFSR keystream, the data falls to an attacker who knows only the 18-byte header: 62 keystream
bits give back the register, the key and every byte. A Geffe combiner of three registers raises
the linear complexity to \(L_1L_2+L_2L_3+L_3=783\), beyond what the header reveals. Its 3/4
correlation with two of the registers still lets a divide-and-conquer search recover the 59-bit
key in about 2 seconds, and we derive when 144 known bits suffice (\(n>8\ln N\)). Finally, the
long/short eruption sequence has strong Markov structure but a linear complexity profile
indistinguishable from fair coin flips: linear complexity sees only linear structure. New library
pieces are `algorithms::lfsr_sequence`, `linear_complexity_profile`, `expected_linear_complexity`,
`geffe_sequence`, `lfsr_correlation_scan` and `datasets::to_csv`.
)

\toc

> Linear feedback shift registers

A *linear feedback shift register* (LFSR) of length \(L\) holds the last \(L\) bits of a sequence and
produces each new bit as an XOR of some of them:

$$
s_i=c_1s_{i-1}\oplus c_2s_{i-2}\oplus\dots\oplus c_Ls_{i-L},\qquad i\ge L,\quad c_j\in\{0,1\}.
$$
\alttext(s i equals c 1 s i minus 1, XOR c 2 s i minus 2, and so on up to c L s i minus L, for i at least L, with each c j equal to 0 or 1.)

The taps are summarised by the *connection polynomial* \(C(x)=1+c_1x+\dots+c_Lx^L\) over
\(\mathrm{GF}(2)\). In generating-function form, with \(S(x)=\sum_i s_ix^i\), the recurrence says that
every coefficient of \(C(x)S(x)\) from \(x^L\) upwards vanishes:

$$
C(x)\,S(x)=P(x),\qquad \deg P<L,\qquad\text{so}\qquad S(x)=\frac{P(x)}{C(x)}.
$$
\alttext(C of x times S of x equals P of x, with the degree of P below L, so S of x equals P of x over C of x.)

An LFSR sequence is a rational power series, and the seed \(s_0,\dots,s_{L-1}\) is encoded in
\(P\). When \(C\) is *primitive* of degree \(L\), every non-zero seed runs through all \(2^L-1\)
non-zero states before repeating, so a 31-stage register has period about \(2.1\times10^9\).
Such *m-sequences* pass most statistical randomness tests: they are balanced, and runs of
each length occur in the expected proportions. That made them the classic keystream generator,
and the *linear complexity* \(L(s)\), the length of the shortest LFSR that generates a sequence,
the natural measure of how weak a keystream is.

> The algorithm

The Berlekamp–Massey algorithm (Berlekamp 1968, Massey 1969) finds the shortest LFSR for
\(s_0,\dots,s_{n-1}\) in a single pass, in \(O(n^2)\) operations.

>> Massey's lemma

Suppose an LFSR of length \(L\) generates \(s_0,\dots,s_{n-1}\) but predicts \(s_n\) wrongly. Then
every LFSR that generates \(s_0,\dots,s_n\) has length at least \(n+1-L\).

The proof is short. Let the second register have length \(L'\) and suppose \(L'\le n-L\). Write
\(a_i=\sum_jc_js_{i-j}\) for the first register's prediction and \(a'_i\) for the second's. For
\(i\le n\) the second register is exact, so \(s_i=a'_i\), and for \(L\le i<n\) the first one is,
so \(s_i=a_i\). Then

$$
a_n=\sum_{j=1}^{L}c_js_{n-j}=\sum_{j=1}^{L}c_j\sum_{k=1}^{L'}c'_ks_{n-j-k}
=\sum_{k=1}^{L'}c'_k\sum_{j=1}^{L}c_js_{n-k-j}=\sum_{k=1}^{L'}c'_ks_{n-k}=s_n,
$$
\alttext(a n equals the sum over j of c j s n minus j, which equals the double sum over j and k of c j c prime k s n minus j minus k, which equals the sum over k of c prime k s n minus k, which equals s n.)

where the inner substitutions are valid because \(n-j\ge L'\) and \(n-k\ge L\) when \(L+L'\le n\). So
the first register predicts \(s_n\) correctly after all, a contradiction. Writing \(L_n\) for the
linear complexity of the first \(n\) bits, the lemma gives

$$
L_{n+1}=\max\big(L_n,\;n+1-L_n\big)\quad\text{whenever the shortest register for } s_0..s_{n-1}\text{ fails at } s_n.
$$
\alttext(L n plus 1 equals the maximum of L n and n plus 1 minus L n, whenever the shortest register for the first n bits fails at bit n.)

Two consequences matter below. A sequence with \(L(s)=L\) is fixed by any \(2L\) consecutive
bits: two different registers of length at most \(L\) cannot agree on \(2L\) bits. And the
complexity can only jump when \(2L_n\le n\).

>> Correcting the register

At step \(n\) the algorithm computes the *discrepancy* \(d=s_n\oplus\sum_{j=1}^{L}c_js_{n-j}\). If
\(d=0\) the current register \(C\) still works. If \(d=1\), it adds a shifted copy of \(B\), the
register saved just before the last length change, at step \(n-m\), whose own discrepancy was
also 1:

$$
C(x)\;\leftarrow\;C(x)+x^{m}B(x).
$$
\alttext(C of x is replaced by C of x plus x to the m times B of x.)

The shifted copy of \(B\) predicts \(s_{n}\) with the same failure that \(B\) made at step \(n-m\),
and agrees with the sequence elsewhere, so the two errors cancel. The new length is
\(\max(L,n+1-L)\), which Massey's lemma shows is the least possible. If the length grew, the old \(C\)
becomes the new \(B\). Over a general field the update is \(C\leftarrow C-(d/b)x^mB\), with \(b\) the
discrepancy saved with \(B\). This is the form used to decode BCH and Reed–Solomon codes, where
the sequence is the syndromes and \(C\) is the error-locator polynomial.

```
BerlekampMassey(s_0..s_{n-1}):
    C = 1, B = 1, L = 0, m = 1
    for i in 0..n-1:
        d = s_i XOR (XOR over j=1..L of c_j s_{i-j})
        if d == 0:         m = m + 1
        else if 2L <= i:   T = C;  C = C + x^m B;  L = i + 1 - L;  B = T;  m = 1
        else:              C = C + x^m B;  m = m + 1
    return L, C
```

The register length after step \(i\) is \(L_{i+1}\), so one pass gives the whole *linear
complexity profile* \(L_1,\dots,L_n\). `algorithms::linear_complexity_profile` returns it.

>> The profile of a random sequence

If the bits are independent and fair, each discrepancy is a fair coin independent of the past,
because \(s_n\) is. Suppose the length has just jumped to \(L\) at step \(i_0\), so
\(L\ge(i_0+1)/2\). Steps before \(2L\) cannot change the length. The first step \(i\ge 2L\) with
\(d=1\) has \(i=2L+j\) with probability \(2^{-(j+1)}\), and moves the length to \(i+1-L=L+j+1\).
The jumps \(K=j+1\) are therefore independent with

$$
\Pr(K=k)=2^{-k},\qquad k=1,2,\dots,\qquad \mathbb E[K]=2,
$$
\alttext(The probability that the jump K equals k is 2 to the minus k, for k equal to 1, 2, and so on; the expected jump is 2.)

and after each jump the profile returns to the line \(L=n/2\). Rueppel (1986) summed this to

$$
\mathbb E[L_n]=\frac n2+\frac{4+(n\bmod 2)}{18}-2^{-n}\Big(\frac n3+\frac29\Big),\qquad
\operatorname{Var}[L_n]\to\frac{86}{81}.
$$
\alttext(The expected L n equals n over 2, plus 4 plus n mod 2 over 18, minus 2 to the minus n times n over 3 plus 2 over 9. The variance of L n tends to 86 over 81.)

A random sequence therefore tracks \(n/2\) to within a bit or two. The test suite checks the
formula against all \(2^n\) sequences for \(n\le 12\). A sequence from a short register instead
stops at its length \(L\) once \(n\ge 2L\).

> Breaking an LFSR stream cipher

A stream cipher XORs the message with a keystream: \(c_i=m_i\oplus k_i\). Our message is
`dm::datasets::faithful()` written as CSV text with `datasets::to_csv`. Its 272 Old Faithful
eruptions, durations and waiting times, make 2,271 bytes, or 18,168 bits. The keystream is
an LFSR with the primitive trinomial \(C(x)=1+x^3+x^{31}\) whose 31-bit secret key is the initial
state. Brute force would try \(2^{31}\) keys. Instead, the attacker guesses that the file
starts with the header `eruptions,waiting` and a newline, 18 bytes. XORing these with the first
144 ciphertext bits gives 144 keystream bits. Berlekamp–Massey needs only \(2L=62\) of them.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/berlekamp_massey_keystream.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

// Connection polynomial 1 + x^t1 + ... + x^degree.
std::vector<int> connection(int degree, std::vector<int> taps) {
  std::vector<int> c(degree + 1, 0);
  c[0] = c[degree] = 1;
  for (int t : taps) c[t] = 1;
  return c;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto faithful = dm::datasets::faithful();
  const std::string plain = dm::datasets::to_csv(faithful);
  const auto message = alg::bytes_to_bits(plain);

  // Encrypt: keystream from the 31-stage register, key = its initial state.
  const auto C = connection(31, {3});
  std::mt19937_64 rng(1968);
  std::vector<int> key(31);
  for (auto& bit : key) bit = static_cast<int>(rng() & 1);
  const auto cipher = alg::xor_bits(message, alg::lfsr_sequence(C, key, message.size()));

  // Attack: known header -> 144 keystream bits -> shortest register -> decrypt everything.
  const auto known = alg::xor_bits(cipher, alg::bytes_to_bits("eruptions,waiting\n"));
  const auto decrypt_with = [&](std::size_t k) {
    const auto fit = alg::berlekamp_massey(std::vector<int>(known.begin(), known.begin() + k));
    const std::vector<int> seed(known.begin(), known.begin() + fit.length);
    const auto guess = alg::xor_bits(cipher, alg::lfsr_sequence(fit.polynomial, seed, cipher.size()));
    return std::pair{fit, alg::bits_to_bytes(guess)};
  };
  const auto byte_accuracy = [&](const std::string& text) {
    std::size_t same = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < plain.size(); ++i) same += text[i] == plain[i];
    return static_cast<double>(same) / static_cast<double>(plain.size());
  };

  std::print("Message: {} bytes, {} bits. Known keystream bits: {}.\n\n", plain.size(), message.size(), known.size());
  std::print("| known bits \\(k\\) | \\(L_k\\) | register found | bytes decrypted correctly |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t k : {16, 32, 48, 60, 61, 62, 64, 144}) {
    const auto [fit, text] = decrypt_with(k);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.1f}% |\n", k, fit.length,
               fit.polynomial == C ? "the true \\(C(x)\\)" : "wrong", 100.0 * byte_accuracy(text));
  }
  std::size_t needed = 0;
  for (std::size_t k = 1; k <= known.size() && needed == 0; ++k)
    if (decrypt_with(k).second == plain) needed = k;
  const auto [fit, text] = decrypt_with(known.size());
  std::print("\nFewest known bits that decrypt the whole file: {}. Recovered key equals the secret key: {}.\n",
             needed, std::vector<int>(known.begin(), known.begin() + 31) == key ? "yes" : "no");
  std::print("\nThe decrypted file parsed back with `datasets::parse_csv` (first rows):\n\n{}",
             dm::datasets::parse_csv(text).head(4).to_md());

  const auto profile = alg::linear_complexity_profile(known);
  std::vector<double> k, Lk, half;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < profile.size(); ++i) {
    k.push_back(i + 1.0);
    Lk.push_back(profile[i]);
    half.push_back((i + 1.0) / 2.0);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(k, half, "n/2 (random bits)", dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5)
      .lines(k, Lk, "keystream profile", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.5)
      .abline_v(62, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0)
      .text(76, 8, "2L = 62 bits", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 13)
      .y_limits(0, 80)
      .title("Linear complexity profile of the known keystream bits")
      .x_label("known keystream bits k")
      .y_label("linear complexity L_k")
      .size(820, 430)
      .save_svg("build/berlekamp_massey_keystream.svg");
}
```
