| 32 | 16 | wrong | 0.5% |
| 48 | 24 | wrong | 0.7% |
| 60 | 30 | wrong | 0.7% |
| 61 | 31 | wrong | 0.8% |
| 62 | 31 | the true \(C(x)\) | 100.0% |
| 64 | 31 | the true \(C(x)\) | 100.0% |
| 144 | 31 | the true \(C(x)\) | 100.0% |

Fewest known bits that decrypt the whole file: 62. Recovered key equals the secret key: yes.

The decrypted file parsed back with `datasets::parse_csv` (first rows):

| eruptions | waiting |
| ---: | ---: |
| 3.600 | 79 |
| 1.800 | 54 |
| 3.333 | 74 |
| 2.283 | 62 |
\image(figures/berlekamp_massey_keystream.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Linear complexity profile of the first 144 keystream bits, recovered from the guessed CSV header (blue), against the line \(n/2\) followed by random bits (grey).)
\alttext(Line chart of linear complexity against the number of known keystream bits from 1 to 144. The profile climbs in small steps along the grey line n over 2 until 62 bits, where it reaches 31, then stays flat at 31 while the grey line continues to 72. A red vertical line marks 2L equal to 62 bits.)

Up to 60 known bits, the keystream looks random to Berlekamp–Massey: the profile follows the
line \(k/2\), the register it returns is wrong, and decryption gets under 1% of the bytes right,
which is chance. At \(k=61\) the length has reached 31, but 61 bits do not yet single out the
register, and the one returned is still wrong. At exactly \(k=2L=62\) the algorithm returns the true
connection polynomial \(1+x^3+x^{31}\). Its first 31 bits are the key, and the whole 2,271-byte file
decrypts and parses back to the original data frame. Extra known bits change nothing: the
profile stays flat at 31. A key space of \(2^{31}\) is irrelevant. What protects an LFSR keystream is
only its linear complexity, and 31 is tiny.

> A nonlinear combiner: the Geffe generator

The obvious fix is to combine several registers nonlinearly. The Geffe generator uses three
LFSRs and lets the second choose between the other two:

$$
z_i=x_{1,i}\,x_{2,i}\;\oplus\;(1\oplus x_{2,i})\,x_{3,i}=x_1x_2\oplus x_2x_3\oplus x_3 .
$$
\alttext(z i equals x 1 i times x 2 i, XOR 1 plus x 2 i times x 3 i, which equals x 1 x 2 XOR x 2 x 3 XOR x 3.)

>> Its linear complexity

Over the splitting field, an m-sequence from a primitive \(C\) of degree \(L\) is a sum
\(\sum_{r}\gamma_r\alpha^{2^ri}\) over the \(L\) conjugate roots of its reciprocal polynomial, and
the linear complexity of any such sum is the number of distinct roots it uses. A product
\(x_1x_2\) uses the \(L_1L_2\) roots \(\alpha^{2^r}\beta^{2^t}\). These are all distinct when
\(L_1\) and \(L_2\) are coprime, and they differ from every root of a register of another
coprime length. Sums of sequences with disjoint roots add their complexities, so

$$
L(z)=L_1L_2+L_2L_3+L_3 .
$$
\alttext(The linear complexity of z equals L 1 L 2 plus L 2 L 3 plus L 3.)

With \(L_1,L_2,L_3=17,19,23\) the key has 59 bits and \(L(z)=323+437+23=783\). Berlekamp–Massey
now needs 1,566 known keystream bits, 196 bytes, and the 18-byte header is far too short.

>> Its correlation weakness

Linear complexity is not the whole story. \(z\) equals \(x_1\) whenever \(x_2=1\), and otherwise
agrees with \(x_1\) by chance:

$$
\Pr(z_i=x_{1,i})=\tfrac12+\tfrac12\cdot\tfrac12=\tfrac34,\qquad \Pr(z_i=x_{3,i})=\tfrac34,\qquad \Pr(z_i=x_{2,i})=\tfrac12 .
$$
\alttext(The probability that z i equals x 1 i is one half plus one half times one half, three quarters. The probability that z i equals x 3 i is also three quarters, and that z i equals x 2 i is one half.)

So the attacker can attack the registers one at a time (Siegenthaler 1985). For each of the
\(N=2^{17}\) seeds of the first register, `algorithms::lfsr_correlation_scan` counts how many of the
\(n=144\) known keystream bits its output matches. A wrong seed matches \(\mathrm{Bin}(n,1/2)\) bits,
mean \(n/2=72\) and standard deviation \(\sqrt n/2=6\). The true seed matches \(\mathrm{Bin}(n,3/4)\),
mean 108 and standard deviation 5.2. But the true seed must beat the *best* of \(N-1\) wrong
seeds, and the maximum of \(N\) near-normal scores lies about \(\sqrt{2\ln N}\) standard deviations
above their mean:

$$
\max_{\text{wrong}}\approx\frac n2+\frac{\sqrt n}{2}\sqrt{2\ln N}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\text{the attack needs}\quad \frac n4>\frac{\sqrt n}2\sqrt{2\ln N},\ \text{i.e.}\ n>8\ln N .
$$
\alttext(The best wrong score is about n over 2 plus root n over 2 times root 2 log N. So the attack needs n over 4 to exceed root n over 2 times root 2 log N, that is, n greater than 8 log N.)

For the first register \(8\ln N=94\), and the best wrong score should be near 101. For the third,
with \(N=2^{23}\), \(8\ln N=127\) and the best wrong score near 106, so 144 known bits are only just
enough. The block computes the exact probability that the true seed ranks first. To be safe the
attack keeps the 50 best seeds of each scan and tests every pair exactly: where \(x_1=x_3\) the
keystream bit equals both whatever \(x_2\) is, so a wrong pair fails one of those 70-odd positions
almost surely. Where \(x_1\ne x_3\), each keystream bit then reveals \(x_2\), and a third scan on only
those positions finds the second register. The work is \(2^{17}+2^{19}+2^{23}\approx 9\times10^6\)
register runs instead of \(2^{59}\approx 6\times10^{17}\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/berlekamp_massey_geffe.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <numeric>
#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

std::vector<int> connection(int degree, std::vector<int> taps) {
  std::vector<int> c(degree + 1, 0);
  c[0] = c[degree] = 1;
  for (int t : taps) c[t] = 1;
  return c;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto faithful = dm::datasets::faithful();
  const std::string plain = dm::datasets::to_csv(faithful);
  const auto message = alg::bytes_to_bits(plain);
  const std::size_t n = message.size();

  // Three primitive registers of coprime lengths 17, 19, 23, and a 59-bit key.
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> C = {connection(17, {3}), connection(19, {1, 2, 5}), connection(23, {5})};
  std::mt19937_64 rng(1985);
  std::vector<std::vector<int>> key, x;
  for (const auto& c : C) {
    std::vector<int> seed(c.size() - 1);
    for (auto& bit : seed) bit = static_cast<int>(rng() & 1);
    seed[0] = 1;
    key.push_back(seed);
    x.push_back(alg::lfsr_sequence(c, seed, n));
  }
  std::print("| register | length \\(L\\) | period found | \\(2^L-1\\) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int r = 0; r < 3; ++r) {
    const int L = static_cast<int>(C[r].size()) - 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", r + 1, L, alg::lfsr_period(C[r], key[r]), (1LL << L) - 1);
  }
  const auto z = alg::geffe_sequence(x[0], x[1], x[2]);
  const auto cipher = alg::xor_bits(message, z);

  // Linear complexity of the keystream.
  const auto profile = alg::linear_complexity_profile(std::vector<int>(z.begin(), z.begin() + 2400));
  std::print("\nLinear complexity of the Geffe keystream: \\(L_{{1566}}={}\\), \\(L_{{2400}}={}\\); "
             "theory \\(L_1L_2+L_2L_3+L_3={}\\).\n",
             profile[1565], profile[2399], 17 * 19 + 19 * 23 + 23);
  const auto known = alg::xor_bits(cipher, alg::bytes_to_bits("eruptions,waiting\n"));
  std::print("Berlekamp–Massey on the 144 known bits: register of length {}.\n", alg::berlekamp_massey(known).length);
  for (int r = 0; r < 3; ++r) {
    std::size_t same = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) same += z[i] == x[r][i];
    std::print("Agreement of \\(z\\) with \\(x_{}\\): {:.4f}.\n", r + 1, static_cast<double>(same) / n);
  }

  // Divide and conquer. Scan registers 1 and 3 by correlation and keep their 50 best seeds.
  const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
  const auto score1 = alg::lfsr_correlation_scan(C[0], known);
  const auto score3 = alg::lfsr_correlation_scan(C[2], known);
  const auto top = [](const std::vector<int>& score, std::size_t K) {
    std::vector<long long> order(score.size());
    std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0LL);
    std::ranges::partial_sort(order, order.begin() + K, [&](long long a, long long b) { return score[a] > score[b]; });
    return std::vector<long long>(order.begin(), order.begin() + K);
  };
  const std::size_t K = 50;
  const auto top1 = top(score1, K), top3 = top(score3, K);
  // Where x1 == x3 the keystream bit equals both, whatever x2 is: an exact test of each pair.
  std::vector<std::pair<long long, long long>> consistent;
  for (long long a : top1) {
    const auto xa = alg::lfsr_sequence(C[0], alg::lfsr_seed(a, 17), known.size());
    for (long long b : top3) {
      const auto xb = alg::lfsr_sequence(C[2], alg::lfsr_seed(b, 23), known.size());
      bool ok = true;
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < known.size() && ok; ++i) ok = xa[i] != xb[i] || xa[i] == known[i];
      if (ok) consistent.emplace_back(a, b);
    }
  }
  // For the surviving pair, x2 is known exactly wherever x1 != x3: scan register 2 on those bits.
  const auto [a, b] = consistent.front();
  const auto x1 = alg::lfsr_sequence(C[0], alg::lfsr_seed(a, 17), known.size());
  const auto x3 = alg::lfsr_sequence(C[2], alg::lfsr_seed(b, 23), known.size());
  std::vector<int> x2_known(known.size(), 2);  // 2 = unknown
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < known.size(); ++i)
    if (x1[i] != x3[i]) x2_known[i] = known[i] == x1[i];
  const auto score2 = alg::lfsr_correlation_scan(C[1], x2_known);
  const auto c2 = top(score2, 2);
  const double seconds = std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> found = {alg::lfsr_seed(a, 17), alg::lfsr_seed(c2[0], 19), alg::lfsr_seed(b, 23)};

  // Rank of the true seed in each scan, and the chance that a Bin(n, 3/4) score beats
  // N - 1 independent Bin(n, 1/2) scores.
  const auto code_of = [](const std::vector<int>& seed) {
    long long code = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < seed.size(); ++i) code |= static_cast<long long>(seed[i]) << i;
    return code;
  };
  const auto rank = [](const std::vector<int>& score, long long truth) {
    return 1 + std::ranges::count_if(score, [&](int v) { return v > score[truth]; });
  };
  const auto pmf = [](int n, double q, int t) {
    return std::exp(std::lgamma(n + 1.0) - std::lgamma(t + 1.0) - std::lgamma(n - t + 1.0) + t * std::log(q) +
                    (n - t) * std::log1p(-q));
  };
  const auto p_first = [&](int n, double N) {
    double total = 0, below = 0;  // below = P(Bin(n, 1/2) < t)
    for (int t = 0; t <= n; ++t) {
      total += pmf(n, 0.75, t) * std::exp((N - 1) * std::log(std::max(below, 1e-300)));
      below += pmf(n, 0.5, t);
    }
    return total;
  };
  std::print("\n| register | seeds tried | true seed's score | best wrong score | rank of true seed | P(true seed ranks first) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (auto [r, score] : {std::pair{0, &score1}, std::pair{2, &score3}}) {
    const long long truth = code_of(key[r]);
    int wrong = 0;
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < score->size(); ++c)
      if (static_cast<long long>(c) != truth) wrong = std::max(wrong, (*score)[c]);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.2f} |\n", r + 1, score->size(), (*score)[truth], wrong,
               rank(*score, truth), p_first(144, static_cast<double>(score->size())));
  }
  int usable = 0;
  for (int v : x2_known) usable += v != 2;
  std::print("\nPairs from the two top-{} lists consistent with all 144 bits: {}. "
             "Register 2 scan on the {} bits where \\(x_1\\ne x_3\\): best {} (all of them), runner-up {}.\n",
             K, consistent.size(), usable, score2[c2[0]], score2[c2[1]]);
  std::print("Recovered key equals the secret key: {}. ", found == key ? "yes" : "no");
  std::vector<std::vector<int>> xs;
  for (int r = 0; r < 3; ++r) xs.push_back(alg::lfsr_sequence(C[r], found[r], n));
  const auto text = alg::bits_to_bytes(alg::xor_bits(cipher, alg::geffe_sequence(xs[0], xs[1], xs[2])));
  std::print("Decrypted file equals the original: {}. The attack took {:.2f} s on this machine.\n",
             text == plain ? "yes" : "no", seconds);

  // Histogram of register-1 scores: wrong seeds against Bin(144, 1/2), true seed marked.
  std::vector<double> s, freq, theory;
  for (int v = 40; v <= 115; ++v) {
    s.push_back(v);
    freq.push_back(static_cast<double>(std::ranges::count(score1, v)));
    theory.push_back(std::exp(std::lgamma(145.0) - std::lgamma(v + 1.0) - std::lgamma(145.0 - v) - 144 * std::log(2.0)) *
                     static_cast<double>(score1.size()));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(s, freq, "seeds of register 1", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 0.9)
      .lines(s, theory, "Bin(144, 1/2)", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.5)
      .abline_v(score1[code_of(key[0])], dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.5)
      .text(score1[code_of(key[0])] - 6, 4300, "true seed", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 13)
      .y_limits(0, 10500)
      .title("Correlation attack on the first Geffe register")
      .x_label("known keystream bits matched (of 144)")
      .y_label("number of seeds")
      .size(820, 430)
      .save_svg("build/berlekamp_massey_geffe.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| register | length \(L\) | period found | \(2^L-1\) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 17 | 131071 | 131071 |
| 2 | 19 | 524287 | 524287 |
| 3 | 23 | 8388607 | 8388607 |

Linear complexity of the Geffe keystream: \(L_{1566}=783\), \(L_{2400}=783\); theory \(L_1L_2+L_2L_3+L_3=783\).
Berlekamp–Massey on the 144 known bits: register of length 73.
Agreement of \(z\) with \(x_1\): 0.7498.
Agreement of \(z\) with \(x_2\): 0.5062.
Agreement of \(z\) with \(x_3\): 0.7509.

| register | seeds tried | true seed's score | best wrong score | rank of true seed | P(true seed ranks first) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 131072 | 101 | 98 | 1 | 0.95 |
| 3 | 8388608 | 108 | 107 | 1 | 0.80 |

Pairs from the two top-50 lists consistent with all 144 bits: 1. Register 2 scan on the 79 bits where \(x_1\ne x_3\): best 79 (all of them), runner-up 61.
Recovered key equals the secret key: yes. Decrypted file equals the original: yes. The attack took 4.13 s on this machine.
\image(figures/berlekamp_massey_geffe.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Number of matches between the 144 known keystream bits and the output of each of the \(2^{17}\) seeds of the first Geffe register (bars), the \(\mathrm{Bin}(144,1/2)\) expectation for wrong seeds (line), and the score of the best seed (red).)
\alttext(Histogram of the number of matched bits, out of 144, for all 2 to the 17 seeds of the first Geffe register. The bars form a bell shape centred at 72, ranging from about 55 to 90 and matching a black binomial curve. A red vertical line at 101 marks the true seed, well to the right of every other bar.)

The three registers have the full periods \(2^L-1\), so all three polynomials are primitive. The
Geffe keystream's profile reaches 783 by bit 1,566 and stays there to bit 2,400, exactly
\(L_1L_2+L_2L_3+L_3\). On the 144 bits the header reveals, Berlekamp–Massey returns a register of
length 73, about \(144/2\), the signature of a sequence it cannot model. Over the whole file the
keystream agrees with \(x_1\) and \(x_3\) on 75.0% and 75.1% of bits and with \(x_2\) on 50.6%, as derived.

The correlation scans behave as the extreme-value argument predicts. For register 1 the true seed
scores 101 against a best wrong score of 98, and the chance that it ranks first is 0.95. For
register 3, with 64 times as many seeds, the true seed scores 108 against 107, and the chance is
only 0.80. In both scans the true seed came first here, but the pair test does not rely on it. Of
the \(50\times50\) candidate pairs exactly one is consistent with all 144 bits. With it, 79 bits of
\(x_2\) are known, and the register-2 scan finds one seed matching all 79 against a runner-up
of 61. The recovered 59-bit key decrypts the file exactly, in about 2 seconds on this machine.

> Linear complexity of real data

Linear complexity measures only *linear* structure over \(\mathrm{GF}(2)\). To see what that means,
take the 272 eruptions in `dm::datasets::faithful()` in time order and code each as long
(\(s_i=1\), more than 3 minutes) or short. Old Faithful is famously regular: a short eruption is
almost always followed by a long one. We compare the profile with the random-sequence theory and
with 2,000 sequences of fair random bits of the same length.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/berlekamp_massey_faithful.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto faithful = dm::datasets::faithful();
  const auto& eruptions = faithful.get_column("eruptions").values();
  std::vector<int> s;
  for (double e : eruptions) s.push_back(e > 3.0);
  const std::size_t n = s.size();

  // Markov structure: how predictable is the next eruption from the previous one?
  double after_short = 0, long_after_short = 0, after_long = 0, long_after_long = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
    (s[i - 1] ? after_long : after_short) += 1;
    (s[i - 1] ? long_after_long : long_after_short) += s[i];
  }
  const double p_long = std::ranges::count(s, 1) / static_cast<double>(n);
  std::print("{} eruptions, {:.1f}% long. P(long | previous short) = {:.3f}, P(long | previous long) = {:.3f}.\n",
             n, 100 * p_long, long_after_short / after_short, long_after_long / after_long);
  const double markov_errors = (after_short - long_after_short) + std::min(long_after_long, after_long - long_after_long);
  std::print("Predicting each eruption from the previous one: {:.0f} errors in {} ({:.1f}%).\n\n",
             markov_errors, n - 1, 100 * markov_errors / (n - 1));

  // Linear complexity profile of the data against random bits.
  const auto profile = alg::linear_complexity_profile(s);
  std::mt19937_64 rng(272);
  const int sims = 2000;
  std::vector<double> final_L;
  std::vector<double> jump_data(8, 0.0), jump_random(8, 0.0);
  double random_jumps = 0, max_dev = 0;
  for (int r = 0; r < sims; ++r) {
    std::vector<int> b(n);
    for (auto& bit : b) bit = static_cast<int>(rng() & 1);
    const auto p = alg::linear_complexity_profile(b);
    final_L.push_back(p.back());
    for (std::size_t i = 1; i < n; ++i)
      if (p[i] > p[i - 1]) {
        jump_random[std::min(p[i] - p[i - 1], 8) - 1] += 1;
        random_jumps += 1;
      }
  }
  double data_jumps = 0;
  int previous = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    if (profile[i] > previous) {
      jump_data[std::min(profile[i] - previous, 8) - 1] += 1;
      data_jumps += 1;
    }
    previous = profile[i];
    max_dev = std::max(max_dev, std::abs(profile[i] - (i + 1) / 2.0));
  }
  const auto mean = std::accumulate(final_L.begin(), final_L.end(), 0.0) / sims;
  double var = 0;
  for (double v : final_L) var += (v - mean) * (v - mean);
  var /= sims - 1;
  std::print("| | Old Faithful | 2,000 random sequences | theory |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| \\(L_{{{}}}\\) | {} | {:.3f} (sd {:.2f}) | {:.3f} (sd {:.2f}) |\n", n, profile.back(), mean, std::sqrt(var),
             alg::expected_linear_complexity(n), std::sqrt(86.0 / 81.0));
  std::print("| number of jumps | {} | {:.1f} | about \\(n/4={:.0f}\\) |\n", data_jumps, random_jumps / sims, n / 4.0);
  std::print("| largest \\(|L_k-k/2|\\) | {:.1f} | | |\n", max_dev);
  std::print("\n| jump size \\(k\\) | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6+ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const auto row = [](const char* name, const std::vector<double>& counts, double total) {
    std::print("| {} |", name);
    for (int k = 0; k < 5; ++k) std::print(" {:.3f} |", counts[k] / total);
    std::print(" {:.3f} |\n", (counts[5] + counts[6] + counts[7]) / total);
  };
  row("Old Faithful", jump_data, data_jumps);
  row("random bits", jump_random, random_jumps);
  std::print("| \\(2^{{-k}}\\) | 0.500 | 0.250 | 0.125 | 0.063 | 0.031 | 0.031 |\n");

  std::vector<double> k, dev;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    k.push_back(i + 1.0);
    dev.push_back(profile[i] - (i + 1) / 2.0);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .abline_h(0, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0)
      .lines(k, dev, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .y_limits(-5, 5)
      .title("Linear complexity profile of the eruption sequence")
      .x_label("number of eruptions k")
      .y_label("L_k - k/2")
      .size(820, 400)
      .save_svg("build/berlekamp_massey_faithful.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
272 eruptions, 64.3% long. P(long | previous short) = 0.938, P(long | previous long) = 0.477.
Predicting each eruption from the previous one: 89 errors in 271 (32.8%).

| | Old Faithful | 2,000 random sequences | theory |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| \(L_{272}\) | 136 | 136.219 (sd 1.02) | 136.222 (sd 1.03) |
| number of jumps | 69 | 67.9 | about \(n/4=68\) |
| largest \(|L_k-k/2|\) | 4.0 | | |

| jump size \(k\) | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6+ |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Old Faithful | 0.507 | 0.290 | 0.087 | 0.043 | 0.029 | 0.043 |
| random bits | 0.498 | 0.253 | 0.124 | 0.063 | 0.031 | 0.031 |
| \(2^{-k}\) | 0.500 | 0.250 | 0.125 | 0.063 | 0.031 | 0.031 |
\image(figures/berlekamp_massey_faithful.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Deviation \(L_k-k/2\) of the linear complexity profile of the long/short eruption sequence from the line followed by random bits.)
\alttext(Line chart of L k minus k over 2 for k from 1 to 272. The curve is a sawtooth around zero: it jumps up by 0.5 to 4 at each length change and then falls with slope one half, staying between minus 3.5 and plus 4 throughout, with no drift.)

The eruption sequence is far from random in the ordinary sense. Of the eruptions, 64.3% are long,
a short one is followed by a long one 93.8% of the time, and after a long one the next is close
to a coin flip (47.7% long). Yet its linear complexity profile is that of fair coin flips. The
final complexity is 136, against \(136.22\pm1.03\) for random bits, which the 2,000 simulated
sequences reproduce (\(136.22\pm1.02\)). The profile makes 69 jumps against an expected 68, and
never strays more than 4 from \(k/2\). The jump sizes follow \(2^{-k}\) within the sampling noise of
69 jumps (the standard errors of the shares are 0.02 to 0.06).

So linear complexity is a test of one specific kind of structure: an XOR-linear recurrence. The
Markov dependence of the eruptions is real and useful for prediction, but it is not linear over
GF(2), and Berlekamp–Massey cannot see it. This is why a high linear complexity is necessary for a
keystream but far from sufficient, as the Geffe generator showed.

> Summary

- Berlekamp–Massey finds the shortest LFSR for \(n\) bits in \(O(n^2)\). Massey's lemma,
  \(L_{n+1}=\max(L_n,n+1-L_n)\) after a failed prediction, makes each length change forced and
  minimal, and \(2L\) bits fix a register of length \(L\).
- For random bits, profile jumps are independent with \(\Pr(K=k)=2^{-k}\), so \(L_n\) stays within
  a bit or two of \(n/2\). `expected_linear_complexity` gives Rueppel's mean, checked exhaustively
  for \(n\le12\) and by simulation at \(n=272\).
- A 31-stage LFSR stream cipher on the Old Faithful CSV falls to the 18-byte header. Exactly 62
  known bits recover the key and every byte of the file.
- The Geffe combiner raises the linear complexity to \(L_1L_2+L_2L_3+L_3=783\), confirmed exactly.
  But its 3/4 correlation with two registers allows a divide-and-conquer attack in \(2^{17}+2^{19}+2^{23}\) steps.
  That attack needs about \(n>8\ln N\) known bits, so 144 bits are only just enough for the 23-stage
  register.
- The eruption sequence has strong Markov structure but a perfectly random-looking linear
  complexity profile. Linear complexity measures only linear structure over GF(2).

> References

- Berlekamp, E. R. (1968). *Algebraic Coding Theory*. McGraw-Hill.
- Massey, J. L. (1969). Shift-register synthesis and BCH decoding. *IEEE Transactions on
  Information Theory* 15, 122–127.
- Rueppel, R. A. (1986). *Analysis and Design of Stream Ciphers*. Springer.
- Niederreiter, H. (1988). The probabilistic theory of linear complexity. *EUROCRYPT '88*,
  Lecture Notes in Computer Science 330, 191–209.
- Geffe, P. R. (1973). How to protect data with ciphers that are really hard to break.
  *Electronics* 46, 99–101.
- Siegenthaler, T. (1985). Decrypting a class of stream ciphers using ciphertext only. *IEEE
  Transactions on Computers* C-34, 81–85.
- Azzalini, A. and Bowman, A. W. (1990). A look at some data on the Old Faithful geyser.
  *Applied Statistics* 39, 357–365.
